--TEST--
A value whose only named type is `Stringable` renders at all four implicitly converting sites, and a parameter, a local and an element of an `array<Stringable>` each reach the same `toString()` — `rule:classes/stringable`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Stringable
// Nothing below names the class it is holding: the interface is the whole
// static type at every site. What this pins is that one `toString()` serves a
// parameter, a local and a list element, that a child inheriting the contract
// is admitted wherever the interface is asked for, and that the four spellings
// of the conversion agree instead of each growing an answer of its own.
class Note implements Stringable {
    public string $text;

    public function constructor(string $text) {
        $this->text = $text;
    }

    public function toString(): string {
        return "note " . $this->text;
    }
}

// Names neither the interface nor its own `implements`: the contract arrives
// with the class it extends, and `parent::` reaches the member it overrides.
final class Reminder extends Note {
    public function toString(): string {
        return "reminder: " . parent::toString();
    }
}

// Shares no base with `Note`, and stands at the same sites for that reason.
final class Bare implements Stringable {
    public function toString(): string {
        return "bare";
    }
}

final class Show {
    public static function echoed(Stringable $s): void {
        echo $s, "\n";
    }

    public static function joined(Stringable $s): string {
        return "<" . $s . ">";
    }

    public static function interpolated(Stringable $s): string {
        return "{$s}!";
    }

    public static function converted(Stringable $s): string {
        return $s as string;
    }
}

Show::echoed(new Note("a"));
Show::echoed(new Reminder("b"));
Show::echoed(new Bare());
echo Show::joined(new Note("c")), "\n";
echo Show::interpolated(new Reminder("d")), "\n";
echo Show::converted(new Bare()), "\n";

// A local declared at the interface, holding the child, at all four sites.
Stringable $held = new Reminder("e");
echo $held, "|", $held as string, "|", "{$held}", "|", "" . $held, "\n";

// Counted rather than read off a line: a spelling that grew its own answer
// fails here while still printing plausibly above. The named call is the
// fourth, because the interface's member is callable like any other.
Stringable $one = new Note("f");
int $agree = 0;
if (($one as string) == "note f") { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (("" . $one) == ($one as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (("{$one}") == ($one as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if ($one->toString() == ($one as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
echo $agree, "\n";

// One list typed at the interface, holding a class and a child of it.
array<Stringable> $all = [new Note("g"), new Reminder("h")];
foreach ($all as Stringable $each) {
    echo "- ", $each, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
note a
reminder: note b
bare
<note c>
reminder: note d!
bare
reminder: note e|reminder: note e|reminder: note e|reminder: note e
4
- note g
- reminder: note h
